  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
    "lesson_core_move": "Construct a finite-duration version of a continuous-time signal by multiplying it by the shifted-unit-step window $u(t-a)-u(t-b)$ and identifying the interval over which the original signal is retained.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The object to recognize is a **window gate** built from unit steps: $u(t-a)-u(t-b)$. Read this as an on-off factor that is multiplied into another signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "First define the pieces you are switching on. The gate turns on at $t=a$ through $u(t-a)$ and turns off at $t=b$ through $u(t-b)$, so the two shifted steps create a retention interval between those times.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the action directly: keep the original signal in the window and kill it elsewhere by writing $y(t)=x(t)(u(t-a)-u(t-b))$. This is the minimum direct-instruction move in this section.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the simplest base case to verify the pattern. Let $x(t)=1$ and form $y(t)=1\\cdot(u(t)-u(t-b))$. For $t<0$, $u(t)=0$ so the output is zero. For $0\\le t<b$, both switches keep the gate at one and the output is one. For $t\\ge b$, $u(t-b)=1$ and the difference is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)(u(t-a)-u(t-b))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-a)-u(t-b)=1\\text{ for }a\\le t<b,\\;0\\text{ otherwise}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Sanity check the generic result from the same rule: the expression says $y(t)=x(t)$ only on the closed-open interval $a\\le t<b$, and $y(t)=0$ outside it, so the gate enforces both retention bounds and clipping in one multiplication.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Windowing a Signal from $t=0 to t=b$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Build $y(t)=t\\,(u(t)-u(t-b))$ and describe the retained interval and equivalent piecewise form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: the interval should turn on at $t=0$ and turn off at $t=b$, so the window factor is $u(t)-u(t-b)$. This is the one new wrinkle in this step compared with the prior tutorial.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base factor directly and identify where it is active. Since $u(t)-u(t-b)=1$ for $0\\le t<b$ and $0$ otherwise, this window preserves any signal only on that interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t)-u(t-b)=\\begin{cases}1,&0\\le t<b\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the multiplication rule with $x(t)=t$: $y(t)=x(t)(u(t)-u(t-b))=t\\,(u(t)-u(t-b))$. The next step is to split by interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=t\\,(u(t)-u(t-b))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $0\\le t<b$, the window is $1$, so $y(t)=t\\cdot 1=t$. For $t<0$ or $t\\ge b$, the window is $0$, so $y(t)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}t,&0\\le t<b\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the worked result confirms the future question pattern: write the output as $y(t)=t(u(t)-u(t-b))$, then read the retained interval as $0\\le t<b$ and the clipped form as zero elsewhere.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Windowing a Signal from $t=a to t=b$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=\\cos t$, multiply by $u(t-a)-u(t-b)$ and write the exact interval where the cosine component remains.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted retention window. In this step the signal stays active from $t=a$ up to $t=b$, so use the same form as before with both shifts: $u(t-a)-u(t-b)$. This adds exactly one wrinkle from the prior step: a nonzero left shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-a)-u(t-b)=\\begin{cases}1,&a\\le t<b\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the core rule from the previous steps: $y(t)=x(t)(u(t-a)-u(t-b))$. Substitute $x(t)=\\cos t$ to get $y(t)=\\cos t\\,(u(t-a)-u(t-b))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\cos t\\,(u(t-a)-u(t-b))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now isolate interval conditions. Multiply by the window value on each region: on $a\\le t<b$, the window equals $1$, and outside this interval it equals $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}\\cos t,&a\\le t<b\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the retained component is the cosine term on $a\\le t<b$, and all other times are zeroed out. This directly mirrors the planned question form: identify the same interval and express the answer in multiplied or piecewise form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Windowing a Signal Across $t=0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write the windowed signal $y(t)=e^{-t}(u(t+2)-u(t-3))$ and identify where $y(t)$ is nonzero.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is that the interval now crosses the origin. Build the window directly as $u(t+2)-u(t-3)$, which uses a switch-on time at $t=-2$ and a switch-off time at $t=3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the two-step rule separately: $u(t+2)=1$ when $t\\ge -2$, and $u(t-3)=1$ when $t\\ge 3$, otherwise each is $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t+2)-u(t-3)=\\begin{cases}1,&-2\\le t<3\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the product rule from the lesson's core move: $y(t)=x(t)(u(t+2)-u(t-3))$. With $x(t)=e^{-t}$, this is $y(t)=e^{-t}(u(t+2)-u(t-3))$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=e^{-t}(u(t+2)-u(t-3))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then isolate the active region. On $-2\\le t<3$, the window is $1$, so $y(t)=e^{-t}$. Outside that interval, the window is $0$, so $y(t)=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}e^{-t},&-2\\le t<3\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This verifies the desired target form: nonzero content is retained on a time interval that crosses $0$, specifically $-2\\le t<3$, while all other regions are clipped to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Windowing a Signal Over a Time Interval",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Retained Interval from a Windowed Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "From $y(t)=x(t)(u(t-2)-u(t-5))$, identify the window interval and rewrite a clean retained-domain sentence.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is to read a finished window expression instead of constructing one. Here the retention window is $u(t-2)-u(t-5)$, so read the switch times as $t=2$ and $t=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the inverse version of the core rule: the first step $u(t-2)$ turns on at $t=2$, and the second step $u(t-5)$ turns off the window at $t=5$. Sort the events and test the intervals $t<2$, $2\\le t<5$, and $t\\ge 5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t-2)-u(t-5)=\\begin{cases}0,&t<2\\\\1,&2\\le t<5\\\\0,&t\\ge 5\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Attach that support to $x(t)$ only where the window equals one. So $y(t)=x(t)$ for $2\\le t<5$, and $y(t)=0$ otherwise.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=\\begin{cases}x(t),&2\\le t<5\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The clean retained-domain statement is: the original signal is kept on the interval $2\\le t<5$, then fully removed elsewhere.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
